{{NZelectorateShortDesc|current=yes|type=general|region=Auckland}}
'''Takanini''' is an electorate to the [[New Zealand House of Representatives]]. It is located in [[South Auckland]] and was first created for the [[2020 New Zealand general election]]. [[Rima Nakhle]] is the sitting [[Member of Parliament (New Zealand)|Member of Parliament]].

==Population centres==
{{maplink|from=New Zealand Electorates/2020/General/Takanini.map|frame=yes|text=Map of boundaries at the [[2020 New Zealand general election|2020]] and [[2023 New Zealand general election|2023]] general elections}}
The electorate is located in the far south of [[Auckland]], on the edge of the city's built-up area. It stretches from [[Wattle Downs]] in its southwest to Mission Heights in the north.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.vote.nz/map/index.html?id=2&modified=2020110110227 |title= Map of electorate boundaries for the 2020 and 2023 general elections |date=17 April 2020}}</ref> It was created out of eastern parts of the {{NZ electorate link|Manurewa}} electorate around Goodwood Heights and Greenmeadows, a small part of northeastern {{NZ electorate link|Hunua}} around Mission Heights, and a northern section of the {{NZ electorate link|Papakura}} electorate around Takanini and Wattle Downs.<ref name="Commission">{{cite web|url=https://vote.nz/assets/Boundary-Review/REPORT-OF-THE-REPRESENTATION-COMMISSION-2020.pdf |title=Report of the Representation Commission 2020 |date=17 April 2020}}</ref> This was due to rapid population growth in the area caused mainly by the outward expansion of [[Auckland]].<ref name="Commission"/> Continued population growth in [[South Auckland]] saw the electorate 17.7% over quota – the highest in the country – and saw the electorate lose [[Mission Heights]] to [[Botany (New Zealand electorate)|Botany]], part of [[Takanini]] to {{NZ electorate link|Papakura}}, part of [[Wattle Downs]] to {{NZ electorate link|Manurewa}} and [[Clover Park, New Zealand|Clover Park]] to {{NZ electorate link|Otahuhu}}.<ref>{{cite book |title=Report of the Representation Commission 2025 |url=https://elections.nz/assets/pagecomponent-file-files/REPORT-OF-THE-REPRESENTATION-COMMISSION-2025-WEB.pdf |isbn=978-0-473-75233-0 |publisher=Representation Commission |access-date=17 September 2025 |page=19 |date=8 August 2025 }}</ref>

==Electorate profile==
[[South Auckland]] has traditionally been a stronghold for the Labour Party. Takanini, however, was considered to be a marginal seat between [[New Zealand Labour Party|Labour]] and [[New Zealand National Party|National]], as it takes in both Labour-leaning areas and National-leaning areas.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/national/403752/national-could-take-new-flat-bush-electorate-academics |title=National could take new Flat Bush electorate − academics |date=20 November 2019 |access-date= 17 April 2019}}</ref> Initially, the electorate's name was proposed as Flat Bush after the [[Flat Bush|Auckland suburb in the north of the electorate]].<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/117560607/new-south-auckland-electorate-named-flat-bush-proposed-for-2020-election |title= New South Auckland electorate named Flat Bush proposed for 2020 election |date=20 November 2019 |access-date=17 April 2020}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/news/article.cfm?c_id=1&objectid=12286748 |title=South Auckland's newest electorate: Flat Bush |date=17 April 2020 |access-date=18 April 2020}}</ref> After a public consultation period, the name was changed to Takanini after a [[Takanini|suburb in the south of the electorate]].<ref name="Commission"/><ref>{{cite news|last=Small|first=Zane|date=17 April 2020|title=Flat Bush was proposed as New Zealand's new electorate but Takanini triumphed|work=[[Newshub]] |url=https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2020/04/flat-bush-was-proposed-as-new-zealand-s-new-electorate-but-takanini-triumphed.html|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200513162518/https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2020/04/flat-bush-was-proposed-as-new-zealand-s-new-electorate-but-takanini-triumphed.html|url-status=dead|archive-date=13 May 2020|access-date=18 April 2020}}</ref>

